.TH MORSEDEC-AUDIO 1 "2026-10-16" "libmorsedec 0.3.0" "General Commands Manual"
.SH NAME
morsedec-audio \- decode Morse code from audio or I/Q files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B morsedec-audio
.RI [ options ]
.I FILE|-
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B morsedec-audio
decodes Morse code from audio files supported by libsndfile, including WAV,
FLAC, and AIFF. It can also read headerless raw PCM from a file or standard
input. With
.BR -i ,
it interprets a two channel input as interleaved I,Q samples and reports signed
frequency offsets from 0 Hz IF.
.PP
Messages are written to standard output when new message snapshots appear.
By default, output is limited to the selected channel; use
.B -a
to print messages from every tracked channel. Diagnostics and errors are
written to standard error. The input operand
.B -
reads from standard input.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI -p " N"
Maximum processing participants including the caller. Zero (default) selects
automatic channel parallelism; one creates no workers. Automatic mode uses
online
logical CPUs capped by channel capacity, keeping fewer than four runnable
channels serial. Explicit values permit parallel processing from two channels.
FFT and band management remain serial. Automatic worker startup failure falls
back to serial; explicit requests report errors. Small workloads can run faster
with one participant. N must be a nonnegative decimal integer.
.TP
.B -i
Interpret input as interleaved I,Q pairs. Input must have exactly two channels.
For raw input, the default channel count becomes two. The default retained band
is -1200 through 1200 Hz.
.TP
.BI -f " HZ"
Pin decoding to a frequency. For audio this must be positive. For I/Q it is a
signed offset from 0 Hz, and zero pins the IF center. Without this option, the
decoder discovers signals automatically.
.TP
.BI -l " HZ"
Set the lower retained band edge. The default is 200 Hz for audio and -1200 Hz
with
.BR -i .
.TP
.BI -u " HZ"
Set the exclusive upper retained band edge. The default is 1200 Hz.
.TP
.BI -w " WPM"
Set the initial Morse speed estimate. Values from 5 through 80 select an
explicit speed; zero uses model priors.
.TP
.B -a
Print messages from all tracked channels instead of only the selected channel.
.TP
.B -j
Write JSON Lines message snapshots, including revisions, instead of human
readable text.
.TP
.BI -t " FILE"
Write per-frame channel metrics as CSV to
.IR FILE .
The trace file must differ from the input file.
.TP
.BI -c " N"
For multichannel audio, select channel
.I N
(1-based). By default, channels are averaged. This option is unavailable with
.BR -i .
.TP
.BI -F " s16|f32"
Read headerless raw samples as signed 16-bit or IEEE binary32 little-endian
values. Raw format must be specified with this option.
.TP
.BI -r " HZ"
Set the sample rate for raw input. Required with
.BR -F .
The rate is samples per second for audio and I/Q pairs per second with
.BR -i .
.TP
.BI -n " N"
Set the number of interleaved channels in raw input. The default is one for
audio and two with
.BR -i .
.TP
.B -V
Print version, copyright, and warranty information, then exit.
.TP
.B -h
Print a short usage summary, then exit.
.SH INPUT FORMAT
Without
.BR -F ,
the input format is determined by libsndfile. With
.BR -F ,
the input is headerless and little-endian. The
.B s16
format is signed 16-bit PCM; the
.B f32
format is IEEE 754 binary32. Raw input requires both
.B -r
and
.BR -F .
Raw channels are interleaved by frame. The
.B -c
option selects one audio channel; otherwise the channels are averaged.
I/Q input always consists of I then Q values and requires exactly two
channels.
.PP
No resampling is performed. The specified or file-provided sample rate must
match the source. Float input is normalized to the range expected by the
decoder.
.SH EXAMPLES
Decode a WAV file:
.PP
.nf
morsedec-audio recording.wav
.fi
.PP
Decode raw 48 kHz stereo audio, using the first channel and JSON output:
.PP
.nf
morsedec-audio -F s16 -r 48000 -n 2 -c 1 -j - < capture.pcm
.fi
.PP
Decode signed I/Q offsets around a pinned signal:
.PP
.nf
morsedec-audio -i -F f32 -r 48000 -f -700 -a capture.iq
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns 0 after successful decoding and output, or a nonzero status if input,
configuration, decoding, or output fails.
